Copenhagen"],"title":"Modelling and Verification of Secure Exams","type":"Book","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383181","year":"2018","yearsub":"2018"}
{"abstract":"Secure systems for voting, exams, auctions and conference paper management are theorised to address the same problem, that of secure evaluations. In support of such a unifying theory comes a model for Secure Evaluation Systems (SES), which offers innovative common grounds to understand all four groups. For example, all rest on submissions, respectively votes, test answers, bids and papers, which are to be evaluated and ultimately ranked. A taxonomy for all groups is advanced to provide a comparative understanding of the various systems. The taxonomy is built according to the type of submissions and the type of evaluation. The uniformity of the security requirements across all groups offers additional validation, and this is an innovative finding in the direction, currently unexplored, of a common system design. Still, the requirements may variously shape up. For example, while voter privacy is normally required forever, anonymity of the submissions is required until after the marking/evaluation phase for the test answers of an exam, for the (sealed) bids of an auction, and for the papers submitted to a conference.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Bella, Giampaolo","Giustolisi, Rosario"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383066"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Common System Design","Secure Evaluation Systems","Security Requirements","Submissions Evaluation","Taxonomy"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"idea a unifying theory for evaluation systems","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/82383063/essos17.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-383066","identifier":[{"id":"85022331888","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-7a6609cb-8436-4cdc-aa70-a166d7148ee6"],"keyword":["Common System Design","Secure Evaluation Systems","Security Requirements","Submissions Evaluation","Taxonomy"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-FEC1325A7BA8351E3D6A0474BAEE240E"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://sites.google.com/site/sarogiustolisi/essos17.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/82383063/essos17.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2017","yearSub":"2017"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Giampaolo","key":"bellapau","last":"Bella","name":"Bella, Giampaolo","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Rosario","key":"giustolisipau","last":"Giustolisi","locid":"62db65c9-470c-472f-b039-6c4a704ef170","name":"Giustolisi, Rosario","orcid":"0000-0002-2917-9601","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/978-3-319-62105-0_15","eisbn":["9783319621050"],"isbn":["9783319621043"],"issn":["03029743"],"publisher":"Springer","series":"Lecture Notes in Computer Science","status":"p","title":"9th International Symposium on Engineering Secure Software and Systems (ESSoS)"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85022331888"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-7a6609cb-8436-4cdc-aa70-a166d7148ee6"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"IDEA: A Unifying Theory for Evaluation Systems","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383066","year":"2017","yearsub":"2017"}
{"abstract":"We introduce the notion of privacy-preserving verifiability for security protocols. It holds when a protocol admits a verifiability test that does not reveal, to the verifier that runs it, more pieces of information about the protocol’s execution than those required to run the test. Our definition of privacy-preserving verifiability is general and applies to cryptographic protocols as well as to human security protocols. In this paper we exemplify it in the domain of e-exams. We prove that the notion is meaningful by studying an existing exam protocol that is verifiable but whose verifiability tests are not privacy-preserving. We prove that the notion is applicable: we review the protocol using functional encryption so that it admits a verifiability test that preserves privacy according to our definition. We analyse, in ProVerif, that the verifiability holds despite malicious parties and that the new protocol maintains all the security properties of the original protocol, so proving that our privacy-preserving verifiability can be achieved starting from existing security","author_count":3,"contributors":["Giustolisi, Rosario","Iovino, Vincenzo","Lenzini, Gabriele"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384952"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["E exams","Functional encryption","Privacy preserving verifiability","Security protocols","Verifiability test"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"privacy preserving verifiability a case for an electronic exam protocol","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/82388254/main.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384952","identifier":[{"id":"85029424758","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-fa5c331f-1ea4-47b8-be36-922327645033"],"keyword":["E-exams","Functional encryption","Privacy-preserving verifiability","Security protocols","Verifiability test"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-9AE35343D719EC403678019E1B5EB32E"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/82388254/main.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Rosario","key":"giustolisipau","last":"Giustolisi","locid":"62db65c9-470c-472f-b039-6c4a704ef170","name":"Giustolisi, Rosario","orcid":"0000-0002-2917-9601","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Vincenzo","key":"iovinopau","last":"Iovino","name":"Iovino, Vincenzo","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Gabriele","key":"lenzinipau","last":"Lenzini","name":"Lenzini, Gabriele","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.5220/0006429101390150","isbn":["9789897582592"],"pages":"139-150","publisher":"SCITEPRESS Digital Library","status":"p","title":"14th International Conference on Security and Cryptography (SECRYPT)"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85029424758"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-fa5c331f-1ea4-47b8-be36-922327645033"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Privacy-Preserving Verifiability: A Case for an Electronic Exam Protocol","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384952","year":"2017","yearsub":"2017"}
{"abstract":"The term security ceremony describes a technical system extended with its human users. In this paper, we examine the inspection ceremony for the mobile transport ticket in Denmark. We find several security weaknesses that are ascribable to both human and computer components of the ceremony. The main vulnerabilities are due to the design choices of how the visual inspection ceremony is organised and the lack of information that is stored into the 2D barcode. These vulnerabilities allow a ticket holder to travel up to 8 zones with a 2-zone subscription and enable several people to travel with the same subscription. The attack is significant as it can be automated, and rather modest skills are necessary to break the inspection ceremony. We state four principles that aim at strengthening the security of inspection ceremonies and propose an alternative ceremony whose design is driven by the stated principles.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Giustolisi, Rosario"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-381670"],"event":{"acronym":"NordSec","title":"Nordic Conference on Secure IT Systems"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["2D Barcode","Inspection Ceremony","Mobile Transport Ticket","Security Ceremony","Security Vulnerabilities"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"free rides in denmark lessons from improperly generated mobile transport tickets","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/82383235/nordsec_17_.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-381670","identifier":[{"id":"85034239583","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-1b36c9cc-eb7a-445a-ba40-c09af1c3b3f2"],"keyword":["2D Barcode","Inspection Ceremony","Mobile Transport Ticket","Security Ceremony","Security Vulnerabilities"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-C252B8D8FE5187DED3F0701C10917579"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://sites.google.com/site/sarogiustolisi/nordsec17.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/82383235/nordsec_17_.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2017","yearSub":"2017"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Rosario","key":"giustolisipau","last":"Giustolisi","locid":"62db65c9-470c-472f-b039-6c4a704ef170","name":"Giustolisi, Rosario","orcid":"0000-0002-2917-9601","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/978-3-319-70290-2_10","eisbn":["9783319702902"],"issn":["03029743"],"pages":"159-174","publisher":"Springer","series":"Lecture Notes in Computer Science","status":"p","title":"22nd Nordic Conference on Secure IT Systems (NordSec)"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85034239583"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-1b36c9cc-eb7a-445a-ba40-c09af1c3b3f2"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Free Rides in Denmark: Lessons from Improperly Generated Mobile Transport Tickets","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-381670","year":"2017","yearsub":"2017"}
{"abstract":"A recent trend in the construction of security protocols such as voting and certificate management systems is to make principals accountable for their actions. Whenever some principals deviate from the protocol’s prescription and cause the failure of a goal of the system, accountability ensures that the system can detect the misbehaving parties who caused that failure. Accountability is an intuitively stronger property than verifiability as the latter only rests on the possibility of detecting the failure of a goal. A plethora of accountability and verifiability definitions have been proposed in the literature. Those definitions are either very specific to the protocols in question, hence not applicable in other scenarios, or too general and widely applicable but requiring complicated and hard to follow manual proofs. In this paper, we advance formal definitions of verifiability and accountability that are amenable to automated verification. Our definitions are general enough to be applied to different classes of protocols and different automated security verification tools. Furthermore, we point out formally the relation between verifiability and accountability. We validate our definitions with the automatic verification of three protocols: a secure exam protocol, Google’s Certificate Transparency, and an improved version of Bingo Voting. We find through automated verification that all three protocols satisfy verifiability while only the first two protocols meet accountability.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Bruni, Alessandro","Giustolisi, Rosario","Schürmann, Carsten"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384358"],"event":{"acronym":"ISC","title":"Information Security Conference"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Accountability","Automated verification","Security protocols","Verifiability","formal definitions"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"automated analysis of accountability","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/82496614/accountability.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384358","identifier":[{"id":"85035149203","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-d23d44bf-7740-4c1c-a790-ad0d4230aa9a"],"keyword":["accountability","automated verification","formal definitions","security protocols","verifiability"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-1AEA50D1EA387434004FA8715C772E56"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/82496614/accountability.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2017","yearSub":"2017"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Theoretical Computer Science, Programming Logic and Semantics"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Alessandro","key":"brunipau","last":"Bruni","locid":"5c50bbc9-ed3d-417f-ba4d-e484eaf536b3","name":"Bruni, Alessandro","orcid":"0000-0003-2946-9462","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Rosario","key":"giustolisipau","last":"Giustolisi","locid":"62db65c9-470c-472f-b039-6c4a704ef170","name":"Giustolisi, Rosario","orcid":"0000-0002-2917-9601","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Carsten","key":"schürmannpau","last":"Schürmann","locid":"1313519f-2074-41d6-8b79-589648e37211","name":"Schürmann, Carsten","orcid":"0000-0003-4793-0099","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/978-3-319-69659-1_23","eisbn":["9783319696591"],"isbn":["9783319696584"],"issn":["03029743"],"pages":"417-434","publisher":"Springer","series":"Lecture Notes in Computer Science","status":"p","title":"Information Security - 20th International Conference, {ISC} 2017, Ho Chi Minh City, Vietnam, November 22-24, 2017, Proceedings"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85035149203"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-d23d44bf-7740-4c1c-a790-ad0d4230aa9a"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Automated Analysis of Accountability","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384358","year":"2017","yearsub":"2017"}
